---
title: "Three classic patterns, traced"
description: "Binary search, a sliding window and a two-pointer palindrome check, each stepped from its own real trace."
---

Three of the patterns that show up across almost every interview problem set, each
stepped from a real run instead of described in prose.

## Binary search: a shrinking range

`lo`, `mid` and `hi` are three plain integers, each a valid index into the array at every
step, so all three show up as pointer carets at once. Step forward and watch the range
between `lo` and `hi` shrink toward the answer, with `mid` landing squarely in the
middle of whatever is left.

::: {.debug name="search"}
```{python}
#| trace: true
def binary_search(a, target):
    lo, hi = 0, len(a) - 1
    while lo <= hi:
        mid = (lo + hi) // 2
        if a[mid] == target:
            return mid
        if a[mid] < target:
            lo = mid + 1
        else:
            hi = mid - 1
    return -1

binary_search([1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 11], 8)
```
:::

## Sliding window: the longest run without a repeat

The window's two edges are `lo` (the left edge) and `i` (the right edge, driven by the
loop). `seen` maps each character to where it last showed up; it earns no picture of its
own, since a dictionary is not one of the four shapes this page can draw, so watch it in
the variables panel while the boxes above it show the string itself, one character per
box.

::: {.debug name="window"}
```{python}
#| trace: true
def longest_unique(s):
    seen = {}
    lo = 0
    best = 0
    for i, c in enumerate(s):
        if c in seen and seen[c] >= lo:
            lo = seen[c] + 1
        seen[c] = i
        best = max(best, i - lo + 1)
    return best

longest_unique("abcabcbb")
```
:::

## Two pointers: is it a palindrome

`lo` and `hi` start at opposite ends of the string and walk toward each other. Step to
the middle and watch both carets land on the same box, one on top of the other rather
than overlapping.

::: {.debug name="palindrome"}
```{python}
#| trace: true
def is_palindrome(s):
    lo, hi = 0, len(s) - 1
    while lo < hi:
        if s[lo] != s[hi]:
            return False
        lo += 1
        hi -= 1
    return True

is_palindrome("racecar")
```
:::
